    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alp-tegin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alp-tegin.jpg" alt="Alp Tegin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alp-tegin">Alp Tegin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C4%B1narba%C5%9F%C4%B1_5.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vikiçizer, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ghaznavid state arose from the military household of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a>, the Persian dynasty that ruled Khurasan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. In 962 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-tegin">Alp Tegin</a>, a Turkic slave commander who had fallen out with the Samanid court, seized the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> in the mountains of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. After several short-lived successors, the Turkic garrison chose another former slave soldier, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabuktigin">Sabuktigin</a>, as its leader in 977. Ruling nominally as a Samanid governor, Sabuktigin (r. 977–997) extended his power over Bust and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> valley and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapala">Jayapala</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Shahi king, pushing his frontier towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>. He and his son Mahmud also intervened in the Samanids' civil wars, and in 994 Mahmud received command of the army of Khurasan. After Sabuktigin's death Mahmud overthrew his brother Ismail in 998, and in 999, as the Samanid state collapsed between the Ghaznavids and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Qarakhanids</a>, he made himself master of Khurasan. The Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Qadir recognised him with the titles Yamin al-Dawla and Amin al-Milla. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> (r. 998–1030) was among the first Muslim rulers to be widely called sultan, and under him the dynasty, ruling from Ghazni, became the leading power of the eastern Islamic world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ghazni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghazni.jpg" alt="Ghazni" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Citadel_of_Ghazni,_seen_from_Tapa_Sardar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">US State Department (US Embassy in Kabul) a US Government organization, hence PD-USGov applies, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ghaznavid lands were ethnically and religiously diverse. A Turkic military elite ruled over Persian-speaking townspeople and peasants in Khurasan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, Afghan and other hill peoples, and, increasingly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> populations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. The state's wealth came from three sources: the land tax of the irrigated oases of Khurasan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, trade along the routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and above all the plunder of Indian temples and cities. Contemporary historians describe great quantities of gold, jewels, captives and elephants brought back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> after each campaign, and some of this wealth was spent on a great mosque, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> and palaces in the capital. The dynasty were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi school</a> who presented themselves as champions of the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, persecuting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismailis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shia-islam">Shi'is</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutazilism">Mu'tazilites</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Rayy</a>. Yet the court was thoroughly Persian in culture. Persian was the language of court poetry and much of the administration, and Ghazni drew poets such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unsuri">Unsuri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhi-sistani">Farrukhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuchihri">Manuchihri</a>. The heavy taxes needed to pay the army burdened Khurasan, and contemporary sources complain of grasping officials, which helped turn the province against the dynasty when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> arrived. After 1040 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> and the Punjab became increasingly central to the economy and to the life of the dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan.jpg" alt="Battle of Dandanaqan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan">Battle of Dandanaqan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaznaly-seljuk_Dandanaqan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ghaznavid army was a professional force paid from the treasury, built around a core of Turkic slave cavalry (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulams</a></em>) bound personally to the sultan. Alongside them served Indian troops, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> regiments with their own commanders, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylamites">Daylamis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>, and a corps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> taken or bought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, used in Khurasan as well as on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shahs-invasion-of-india">Indian campaigns</a>. Mahmud's expeditions into India, traditionally counted as seventeen between 1001 and 1026, began with the defeat of the Hindu Shahi king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapala">Jayapala</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> in 1001. He took the Ismaili-ruled city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> in 1006 and again in 1010, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anandapala">Anandapala</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Chach</a> in 1008, plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangra-fort">Nagarkot</a>, Thanesar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kanauj</a>, and in 1025–1026 marched across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thar-desert">Thar</a> desert to sack the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somnath-temple">Somnath</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> coast. These were mostly raids for plunder rather than conquests, apart from the annexation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. In the west Mahmud defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Qarakhanids</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> in 1008, annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> in 1017 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Rayy</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> in 1029. The army's weakness appeared under his son Mas'ud I, whose heavy columns, slowed by baggage and elephants, could not pin down the mobile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turkmen</a> horsemen, who finally defeated them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan">Battle of Dandanaqan</a> in 1040.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar.jpg" alt="Ahmad Sanjar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar">Ahmad Sanjar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmad_Sanjar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rashid Al-Din, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ghaznavid government followed the model of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> and, behind them, the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>. The sultan was an absolute ruler whose legitimacy rested on military success, on recognition by the caliph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and on the display of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> orthodoxy. Under him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> headed the civil administration, organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwans</a></em>) for finance, for the army and its pay, for correspondence and for the postal and intelligence service, which reported on officials across the empire. Mahmud's viziers included Abu'l-Abbas Fadl Isfarayini and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-hasan-maymandi">Ahmad ibn Hasan Maymandi</a>, both experienced Persian bureaucrats. Provinces were run by military commanders and civilian officials whose powers were deliberately separated and watched by informers. Court politics were turbulent: on Mahmud's death in 1030 his son Muhammad was proclaimed, but Mas'ud overthrew and blinded him, and in 1032 executed Mahmud's former vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasanak">Hasanak</a>. The detailed history of Mas'ud's reign by the secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-bayhaqi">Abu'l-Fazl Bayhaqi</a> depicts a court divided between the old servants of Mahmud and Mas'ud's own men. After 1040 the later Ghaznavids governed a smaller state of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> with similar institutions; Ibrahim (r. 1059–1099) and Mas'ud III (r. 1099–1115) gave it long, stable reigns, while from 1117 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-shah-of-ghazna">Bahram Shah</a> ruled as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar">Sanjar</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/somnath-temple"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/somnath-temple.jpg" alt="Somnath temple" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/somnath-temple">Somnath temple</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Somanath_mandir_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">B. SurajPatro1997, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ghaznavids had a deep influence on the cultures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. Their court helped make New Persian a language of imperial power far beyond its Iranian homeland. Ferdowsi completed the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a></em>, the national epic of Iran, around 1010 and presented it to Mahmud, although the story that the sultan rewarded him poorly is a later legend that cannot be verified. The scholar al-Biruni, brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> after the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, travelled in the Indian borderlands and wrote his <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-hind">Kitab ta'rikh al-Hind</a></em>, a study of Indian religion, philosophy and science that is among the most remarkable works of medieval scholarship, and dedicated his astronomical treatise <em>al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qanun-al-masudi">Qanun al-Mas'udi</a></em> to Mas'ud. Bayhaqi's history is a classic of Persian prose, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanai">Sana'i</a> dedicated his <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadiqat-al-haqiqa">Hadiqat al-haqiqa</a></em> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-shah-of-ghazna">Bahram Shah</a>. By annexing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> a centre of Persian culture, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> Ali Hujwiri wrote the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashf-al-mahjub">Kashf al-mahjub</a></em>, the Ghaznavids prepared the ground for the Ghurids, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and centuries of Persianate Muslim rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Their raids, above all the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somnath-temple">Somnath</a>, became central to later memory: Muslim chroniclers celebrated Mahmud as a holy warrior, while in modern India the raids became a symbol of iconoclasm, a contested memory examined by the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romila-thapar">Romila Thapar</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tughril"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tughril.jpg" alt="Tughril" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seljuk_Sultan_Tugrul_Bey_sitting_on_his_throne._Topkap%C4%B1_Saray%C4%B1_M%C3%BCzesi_1653-3_(304_B).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1314 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ghaznavid empire was overstretched from the start, and its collapse in the west was rapid. In the 1030s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes led by the Seljuk brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaghri-beg">Chaghri</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a> into Khurasan, where the taxation of Mas'ud's officials had alienated the towns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> opened its gates to Tughril in 1038, and in May 1040 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> crushed Mas'ud's exhausted army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan">Battle of Dandanaqan</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>. Mas'ud withdrew towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where his own troops deposed him and killed him early in 1041. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawdud-of-ghazni">Mawdud</a> avenged him and restored order, but Khurasan and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> were lost for good to the new Seljuk Empire. The eastern state survived for almost a century and a half: Ibrahim made peace with the Seljuks around 1059 and the realm remained rich, but in 1117 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar">Sanjar</a> installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-shah-of-ghazna">Bahram Shah</a> as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. A feud with the rising Ghurid chiefs of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> proved fatal. Avenging two brothers put to death by Bahram Shah, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-din-husayn">Ala al-Din Husayn</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghur</a> sacked and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> in 1150 or 1151, earning the name <em>Jahan-suz</em>, 'world burner'. The Ghaznavids withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, and Ghazni was later held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turkmen</a> and then, from 1173, by the Ghurids. In 1186 Mu'izz al-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a> took Lahore and imprisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khusrau-malik">Khusrau Malik</a>, the last Ghaznavid sultan, ending the dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ghaznavid Empire, founded by Turkic slave soldiers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, joined Khurasan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a>, whose raids opened northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to Muslim conquest and whose court sheltered Ferdowsi and al-Biruni, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and the Ghurids destroyed it.</em></p>

